Responses do not set X-Content-Type-Options or other browser-side security headers
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/argmin-security-review-2026-06-23-l1
Criticality: low (attack path: low)
Status: accepted-risk

# Resolution
Accepted as a deliberate AWS-compatibility boundary. AWS S3 does not set
`X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `Referrer-Policy`, or other browser-side
security headers on its responses; the S3 API surface treats object content
type as user-controlled metadata and leaves the browser-side hardening
expectation to the bucket owner (e.g. via a CloudFront or Lambda@Edge
function, or via storing the right `Content-Type` on each object). The threat
model already documents that "we are building a server that must exactly
match AWS S3 behaviour at all times. Do not work around this, always fix
non conforming behaviour." Adding a response header that AWS itself does not
send would be a deliberate deviation, not a fix, and would risk breaking
S3-compatibility test suites and downstream SDKs that expect the documented
AWS response shape.

The defence is still available to operators without a code change: bucket
owners who want `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` (or any other response
header) on a per-object basis can store it as user metadata that
`apply_user_metadata_headers` will echo back, or front the server with a
proxy/CDN that injects the header. This finding is filed so the gap stays
visible in the inventory and so the rationale for not adding it stays
documented alongside the other accepted boundaries.

