# Summary
`s3_response_to_hyper` (`crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:4635-4810`) and the streaming response builders in `crates/server-http/src/http/response.rs` do not set any browser-side security headers. The `Content-Type` header on `GetObject` responses is taken from the stored object metadata (`crates/server-http/src/http/response.rs:847-870`), so a user uploading a `text/html` object will have that content type served back on GET. Without `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, a browser can MIME-sniff a binary upload whose stored content type is permissive and execute it as HTML/JS. AWS S3 does not set this header on its responses; the convention is that bucket owners add the header themselves when they care, either by storing it as user metadata on each object, by a CloudFront/Lambda@Edge function, or by an external proxy. Adding the header at the server would deviate from the documented AWS response shape and conflict with the project's threat model requirement to match AWS S3 behaviour exactly.

