## Report
Grep for `X-Content-Type-Options`, `nosniff`, `X-Frame-Options`, and `Content-Security-Policy` in `crates/server-http/src/` returns no matches. The S3 XML error responses and streaming PUT/GET responses are both unaffected. AWS S3 does not set `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` on its responses — verified by reading the public S3 response documentation and matching it against the server's current response shape. `AGENTS.md` explicitly says "we are building a server that must exactly match AWS S3 behaviour at all times. Do not work around this, always fix non conforming behaviour" and `guides/threat_model.md` says the server must match AWS S3 API behaviour. Closing the gap operationally: a bucket owner can store the header as `x-amz-meta-x-content-type-options: nosniff` on a specific object (the user metadata echo in `apply_user_metadata_headers` will replay it), or front the server with a CloudFront distribution or a reverse proxy that injects the header. The threat-model text now notes the accepted boundary in the same style as the other accepted risks.

